<% texl.import "~/lib/tutorial/index.texl" ~> (lib -> bind "tutorial" lib.data) %>
<!--{ li01 : rich }-->
# 8. Linking

A document can use the values of another one: a library of materials, the
loads from a colleague's calculation, the rates of a price list. When the
other document changes, yours follows. A document can also link to other
documents, images and files. About five minutes.
<!--{ li02 : markdown }-->
## Import a document

This tutorial has a small library of steel sections,
[sections](lib/steel/sections.texl), in the folder `lib/steel`. Open it,
look at how it gives its values names, and come back with the browser's back
button.

A formula imports a document with `texl.import`, and gives all the names of
that document one name of its own with `bind`.
<!--{ li03 : markdown }-->
**Your turn.** Select the empty line just above the check below, press **=**
for a Formula cell, and write:

`texl.import "~/lib/steel/sections.texl" ~> (lib -> bind "steel" lib.data)`

then press **Ctrl+Enter**. This reads the library and names its values
`steel`: its section IPE 300 is then `steel.IPE.IPE300`. In a project, as
this tutorial is, `~` stands for the project folder, so the same address
works from any folder of it.
<!--{ li04 : rich }-->

<!--{ li05 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check (steel.IPE.IPE300.mass == 42.2) %>
<!--{ li06 : markdown }-->
Then this line uses the library: an **<% steel.IPE.IPE300.name ; "IPE 300" %>** weighs
**<% steel.IPE.IPE300.mass ; "…" %> kg/m**, in steel <% steel.grade.name ; "…" %>.

Now open the library again, change the mass of the IPE 300 to `42.5`, save
with **Ctrl+S**, and come back: this line shows the new mass. Put it back to
`42.2` afterwards.

An address can also be relative to the document, as `lib/steel/sections.texl`
from here, or `../steel/sections.texl` from a document in another folder of
`lib`. In the navigation pane, right-click a document and choose
**Copy link** to get its address from the open document.
<!--{ li07 : markdown }-->
## Links to read

A Markdown link, `[text](address)`, does not import anything: a click opens
what it points to. A link to a document opens it in the editor, like this one
to the [index](index.texl). A link to another file opens it in a new tab,
like this [drawing of the beam](files/beam-drawing.pdf), a PDF.

An image is written as a link with `!` in front, as
`![A beam](files/beam.svg)`:

![A simply supported beam under a uniform load](files/beam.svg)

Try it: on an empty line at the end of this lesson, press **m** for
Markdown and write a link to `files/beam-drawing.pdf`. The navigation pane
shows the PDF in the folder `files`: right-click it and choose **Copy link**
to get its address, then paste it into the link.

More in the manual: [Linking documents](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/editor/linking.html).
